# Differential evolution with local search

`DeInject` combines differential evolution with local refinement of the best
population members. The outer search explores the landscape, while a
configurable inner solver polishes selected points after each generation.

[API reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/de_inject/struct.DeInject.html)

| Property | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Objective | A scalar function with useful local refinement |
| Evaluations | `CostFunction`, plus the inner operator's requirements |
| Search | Population search with fresh local polishing |
| Constraints | A box-only Constraints declaration |
| Parameters | All four dense backends with a compatible inner |

## When to use it

Use this hybrid when local refinement improves useful basins cheaply
enough to justify fewer global samples at the same evaluation budget.
Compare the unmodified outer solver before increasing local work: repeated
polishing of one basin can reduce exploration on a multimodal objective.
For searches that retain each basin's learned local model, compare
[local-search chains](../ma-ls-ch/).

The inner solver determines derivative requirements. Nelder–Mead uses cost
values alone, Levenberg–Marquardt needs residuals and a Jacobian, and
L-BFGS-B uses gradients and a box declaration. Custom inners implement
`MemeticInner`, extending `WarmStart` with a scale-aware seed.

## How it works

After a DE generation, the solver refines the best `k` members. Each local
solve starts fresh. The refined point is clipped to the box and reevaluated;
it replaces its source only on a strict cost improvement. The population
is then sorted again. The current best cost therefore remains non-increasing.

`with_refine_every(n)` schedules refinement at outer iterations
`0, n, 2n, …`; the default is every iteration. The scale hint supplied to the
inner is `1.0`: DE has no distribution step size. Nelder–Mead uses it as
its initial simplex edge; LM and L-BFGS-B ignore it.

[Noman and Iba (2008)](#ref-noman-iba-2008) studied DE with adaptive local
search. Basin accepts a general inner solver instead of reproducing their
specific simplex-crossover operator or adaptive local-search length.

## Example

The sphere has its minimum at the origin, with cost zero. This example
uses a fixed seed and checks the historical best against that analytic
reference. A successful result on this objective does not certify global
optimality on a general problem.

<!-- compile: solver-de-inject -->
```rust
use basin::{
    BoxBounds, Constrained, ConstraintSet, CostFunction, De, DeInject,
    Executor, NelderMead, PopulationProgress, RelaxedInner,
};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct Sphere;

impl CostFunction for Sphere {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok(x.iter().map(|v| v * v).sum())
    }
}

fn main() {
    let bounds = BoxBounds::new(Some(vec![-5.0; 2]), Some(vec![5.0; 2]));
    let problem =
        Constrained::new(Sphere, ConstraintSet::new().with_bounds(bounds));
    let outer = De::new(7).with_pop_size(20);
    let inner = NelderMead::adaptive();
    let solver = DeInject::with_inner_solver(outer, RelaxedInner::new(inner))
        .with_k(1)
        .with_inner_max_iter(50);

    let result =
        Executor::new(problem, solver, PopulationProgress::<Vec<f64>>::empty())
            .max_iter(100)
            .run()
            .unwrap();

    assert!(result.best_cost() < 1e-4);
    assert!(result.best_param().iter().all(|v| v.abs() < 0.01));
}
```

## Practical considerations

### Budget both searches

`with_k` defaults to `1`, and `with_inner_max_iter` defaults to `50` per
local solve. Increasing either can spend most of the evaluation budget on
polishing. Outer `max_iter` counts generations, not local iterations.
Executor budgets are checked between outer steps, so a generation and its
local work can exceed the remaining allowance.

All inner evaluations use the outer problem's counters. `cost_evals()`
counts cost requests alone; gradients, residuals, Jacobians, and Hessian
work keep their own categories. Use
`max_evaluations(EvaluationKind::TotalWork, n)` to budget their sum.
Enable `with_inner_diagnostics(true)` and retain the solver to inspect
`inner_diagnostics()` when local stopping reasons matter.

### Choose a compatible inner solver

The outer requires a complete box-only `Constraints` declaration, with
finite, ordered endpoints of the correct dimension. Additional constraint
blocks are unsupported. Pass a box-respecting inner such as L-BFGS-B
directly so both searches use the same box.

Wrap unconstrained Nelder–Mead or LM in `RelaxedInner::new(inner)` as the
example does. This permits the local objective evaluations to leave the
box. Outer repair protects the published point, but the objective must
remain valid wherever the local solver explores. Use a compatible bounded
inner if evaluating outside the box is invalid.

### Refine sparingly when exploration matters

Increase `with_refine_every`, reduce the inner iteration cap, or add a
per-local-solve cost allowance with `with_ls_intensity`. These controls
limit how often and how long the search exploits its current best region.
They do not implement an adaptive trigger based on population fitness.
Scale coordinates deliberately: the fixed unit scale hint can make a
Nelder–Mead simplex too large or too small for the problem.


## Implementation

`PopulationProgress` publishes population points, objective costs, and the
historical incumbent. The wrapper owns the outer solver and inner executor;
it does not store a persistent chain for each member. Each refinement
initializes its own local run.

Exact solver-and-state checkpoints retain the outer search, inner solver,
progress, RNG, and all six evaluation categories. Fresh runs initialize the
outer search again. With `serde`, serialization requires serializable
constituent solvers and parameters; application hooks require an owned
checkpoint.

Supported parameter types are `Vec<F>`, `nalgebra::DVector<F>`,
`ndarray::Array1<F>`, and `faer::Col<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`, when
the chosen inner supports them. Custom inners can narrow coverage.
The DE outer needs only vector operations.

